    "cost_closing_html":  "About \u003cstrong\u003e$2.41 a bowl\u003c/strong\u003e for a thick, bacon-smoked chowder. Potatoes and canned corn are two of the cheapest things you can buy, and here they are doing most of the heavy lifting.",
    "shop_smart":  [
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eBuy potatoes in the big bag.\u003c/strong\u003e This chowder leans on them hard, and the per pound price on an eight or ten pound bag is a fraction of what loose potatoes cost.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eCanned corn beats frozen for a chowder.\u003c/strong\u003e It is cheaper, it is already soft, and the liquid in the can can go right into the pot instead of down the drain.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eBacon is the flavor engine, so a little goes far.\u003c/strong\u003e Render it first, cook everything else in that fat, and you get smoke through the entire pot from a quarter pound.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eTurkey breast is the one line worth shopping.\u003c/strong\u003e Buy a frozen roast when the holiday price hits, cook it, and freeze it pulled and portioned for chowders and soups all winter."
                   ],
    "make_it":  [
                    "Weigh your empty mixing pot and write the number down for portioning later.",
                    "Chop the bacon and render it in your big pot with the olive oil until crisp. Lift the bacon out and leave the fat.",
                    "Add the butter, then the diced onion and celery, and cook until soft. Stir in the garlic for the last minute.",
                    "Sprinkle the flour over the vegetables and stir for two minutes so it loses the raw taste and coats everything.",
                    "Pour in the chicken broth while stirring, add the peeled and cubed potatoes, and simmer until the potatoes are fork tender and starting to break down.",
                    "Add the drained corn and the cooked turkey breast and bring it back to a simmer.",
                    "Stir in the heavy cream and the hot sauce, warm it through without boiling, then season with salt and black pepper and scatter the reserved bacon back on top."
                ],
